	langid = {english},
	abstract = {Responding to Peggy Noonan's account of the difference between the American political parties, this blog post argues that the conventional view gets two things wrong. First, the parties' key differences are not substantive but rhetorical: Republicans do not want to abolish the welfare state and Democrats do not want socialism, yet each portrays small policy shifts by the other as radical. Second, the parties' rhetoric does not make sense on its own terms: Democrats who truly cared about the poor would focus on the world's absolutely poor, for instance through immigration, and Republicans who truly cared about taxpayers would seek big cuts to popular programs such as Social Security and Medicare. The median voter theorem explains why parties stay near the political center, but not the gap between moderate substance and immoderate rhetoric. The author conjectures that radical rhetoric is a bone thrown to each party's idealists, and perhaps an illusion that moderates also enjoy. – AI-generated abstract.}
}

