	langid = {english},
	abstract = {This briefing note presents the World Bank's updated estimates of absolute consumption poverty in the developing world for 1981--2008, with preliminary estimates for 2010. The estimates draw on over 850 household surveys for almost 130 developing countries, representing 90\% of the developing world's population, with all money values adjusted for inflation and purchasing power, and past estimates revised back to 1981 on a consistent basis. The main poverty line is \$1.25 a day at 2005 prices, supplemented by \$1 and \$2 lines. For the first time since monitoring began, both the poverty rate and the number of poor fell in all six developing regions between 2005 and 2008. The share of the developing world's population below \$1.25 a day fell from 52\% in 1981 to 22\% in 2008, meaning 1.29 billion people lived below that line in 2008, compared with 1.94 billion in 1981. – AI-generated abstract.}
