	abstract = {Differences in lifetime earnings by educational attainment have been of great research and policy interest. Although a large literature examines earnings differences by educational attainment, research on lifetime earnings — total accumulated earnings from entry into the labor market until retirement — remains limited because of the paucity of adequate data. Using data that match respondents in the Survey of Income and Program Participation to their longitudinal tax earnings as recorded by the Social Security Administration, the authors estimate 50-year work-career effects of education on lifetime earnings for men and women. By overcoming the purely synthetic cohort approach, the results provide a more realistic appraisal of actual patterns of lifetime earnings, and show that net-of-controls education premiums are notably smaller than previously reported estimates.},
