	langid = {english},
	abstract = {In this policy forum, a group of 38 scientists warns that mirror bacteria — organisms built entirely from molecules of the opposite chirality to those found in natural life — could pose unprecedented risks to humans, animals, plants and ecosystems. Although the capability to create mirror life is likely at least a decade away, the authors argue that mirror bacteria could evade immune defences and natural predators, potentially spreading through the environment and causing pervasive infections. Summarizing a longer technical report, they conclude that, unless compelling evidence emerges that mirror life would not be dangerous, research aimed at creating mirror bacteria should not proceed. They call for broad discussion among scientists, funders, policymakers and the public to chart a path forward, while preserving beneficial research on mirror-image molecules. – AI-generated abstract.},
