	langid = {english},
	abstract = {This editorial argues that Goodhart's law, popularly phrased as ``when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure,'' and Donald Campbell's related warning that heavily used quantitative social indicators become corrupted apply widely in graduate medical education. It gives examples such as residents being coached to answer the ACGME Resident/Fellow Survey favourably, study focused narrowly on passing USMLE Step 1 when its scores drive selection, and publication counts that encourage salami slicing and honorary authorship. To mitigate these unintended consequences, the authors recommend using logic models to identify which behaviors a metric rewards, preferring criterion-referenced to norm-referenced assessment, adopting a longitudinal and developmental focus, and relying more on narrative assessments that are harder to game. They conclude that educators should anticipate how metrics distort behavior and embrace subjective, narrative approaches to assessment and program evaluation. – AI-generated abstract.},
