	abstract = {This guest post, originally published by Works in Progress as ``The algorithm will see you now'', asks why demand for human radiologists is at an all-time high even though radiology, with its digital images, clear benchmarks and repeatable tasks, looks like an ideal candidate for automation, and hundreds of FDA-cleared AI models now outperform radiologists on narrow benchmarks. It argues that models often work less well outside their test conditions, that regulators and medical insurers have so far been reluctant to approve or cover fully autonomous radiology models, and that image interpretation is only part of what radiologists do, citing a study that found they spend only 36 percent of their time on it. It also suggests that cheaper, faster reading can raise demand for imaging by more than it raises efficiency, an instance of the Jevons paradox. – AI-generated abstract.},
