	langid = {english},
	abstract = {This post is an amalgam of thoughts about death, prompted centrally by Atul Gawande's book \emph{Being Mortal}. Although the book describes much pain and degeneration, its tone feels "smooth", and beneath it one senses an ocean of pain, confusion, fear and despair, which other accounts of death in hospitals portray more starkly. The author reflects on Gawande's thesis that the modern medical system often makes the end of life worse than necessary by focusing on safety and on prolonging life rather than on what patients actually care about. He also reflects on how an awareness of death can bring intimacy, though deathbeds do not guarantee it, and on how the book left him more aware of his body's fleeting health, of the preciousness of relationships and of the costs of wasting time. – AI-generated abstract.},
