	urldate = {2026-11-10},
	langid = {english},
	abstract = {In a debate with Aubrey de Grey, William Hurlbut repeated the standard claim that life without death would be meaningless and empty, without making a sincere effort to think about what meaning immortality would give to life. Philosophers have put great effort into finding nice things to say about death, but not an equally motivated effort into saying nice things about immortality; if everyone were hit on the head with a baseball bat once a week, philosophers would soon discover its benefits. Modern literature about immortality is written by authors who expect to die, and if people were immortal they would find nice things to say about immortality instead. The author concludes that neither death nor immortality is needed to give meaning to life: life gives meaning to life, and virtuous behavior needs no such excuse. – AI-generated abstract.},
